---
# Copy to cee_windows.yml. Applies to every host in the cee_windows group.
#
# Windows Server support is implemented. cee_preflight's OS-family gate
# accepts 'Windows' and its platform gate requires a server edition
# (ansible_os_product_type == 'server'; client editions are rejected by
# name). A single ansible-playbook site.yml run has completed with
# failed=0 against a real Windows Server 2025 Datacenter host, in the
# same play as RHEL and SLES hosts. Two things that run has NOT proven:
# the host was WORKGROUP, not domain-joined, so nothing domain-dependent
# is validated; and its inbound port was never opened, so the network
# path from PowerStore's side is unverified. See
# docs/ansible-deployment.md's Windows section for the full picture.

# Ansible reaches Windows Server over OpenSSH here, not WinRM. The SSH
# server's DefaultShell must be PowerShell for ansible_shell_type below to
# match; if it is left as cmd, set ansible_shell_type: cmd instead.
#
# Note there is no credential delegation over this transport, unlike
# WinRM with CredSSP. It does not matter for this deployment: the
# installer is copied to the host before it runs, so nothing needs a
# second hop to a network share.
ansible_connection: ssh
ansible_shell_type: powershell

# CEE has NO file-based log on Windows. Confirmed by harvest against a
# live CEE 9.2.0.0 (and 9.3.0.0) install on Windows Server 2025: no
# LogFile registry value exists anywhere under HKLM:\SOFTWARE\EMC\CEE,
# and no .log files ever appear on disk. CEE logs exclusively to the
# Windows Application Event Log, under two sources: "EMC CEE" and
# "CEE Monitor". See
# docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-10-cee-windows-releve.md for the full
# harvest.
#
# cee_verify's Windows branch confirms health by querying the Event Log
# (Get-WinEvent -LogName Application -ProviderName 'EMC CEE') rather than
# by finding log files the way the Linux role does. There is no
# cee_log_path variable for Windows — it is asserted Linux-only, in
# cee_preflight/tasks/assert_required_vars_linux.yml, not by the shared
# cee_common gate. Do not add it here.
#
# The service that owns the CEPA listener on 12228 is "EMC Checker
# Server" (display name "EMC CAVA", binary CAVA.exe) — measured on the
# live host, not "EMC CEE Monitor" (CEEMtrSvc.exe) despite the latter's
# name. cee_configure and cee_verify drive EMC Checker Server
# deliberately.

# SSH multiplexing is disabled for Windows hosts on purpose. It works for
# one-off interactive commands, but Ansible drives dozens of PowerShell
# module calls back to back over one connection and the shared
# ControlMaster channel wedges partway through — the symptom is
# "Data could not be sent to remote host ... #< CLIXML" mid-play, reported
# as unreachable rather than as a task failure. Observed against Windows
# Server 2025 with DefaultShell = pwsh.
#
# This overrides whatever ControlMaster setting the operator's
# ~/.ssh/config carries, so their interactive sessions keep multiplexing.
ansible_ssh_common_args: '-o ControlMaster=no -o ControlPath=none'

# Which CEE release these hosts run. BOTH are required — cee_preflight's
# assert_required_vars_windows.yml refuses to continue without them, and
# there is deliberately no default: silently installing a different
# release than the operator believed is the same class of silent success
# the rest of this repo exists to prevent.
#
# They are a pair. win_package decides whether CEE is already installed
# purely from the ProductCode, and the GUID changes with every release,
# so cee_install reads HKLM:\SOFTWARE\EMC\CEE\Version back afterwards and
# asserts it equals cee_windows_version — a half-updated pair fails
# loudly instead of configuring whichever CEE happens to be there.
#
# Known pairs, both read out of a live Uninstall key on a real host
# rather than guessed — docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-10-cee-windows-
# releve.md:
#
#   9.2.0.0  {81F4A925-A885-4F58-8907-641BC7E82B99}   <- vendored in bin/
#   9.3.0.0  {149370D4-461B-43D1-9D8E-71FCBA58A618}
#
# Naming a release bin/ does not carry is supported when the host already
# has it installed: cee_install checks the Uninstall key first, skips
# staging entirely, and goes straight to verifying layout and version.
# That is the normal case for 9.3.0.0 — Dell ships its installer only
# through their authenticated portal, so none is vendored here.
#
# The registry tree is identical between 9.2.0.0 and 9.3.0.0 (measured,
# full diff in the harvest doc above), so cee_configure needs no version
# branch. The only functional difference is the
# Security\Http\ServerEnabled default — 0 in 9.2.0.0, 1 in 9.3.0.0 — and
# cee_configure writes 1 either way.
cee_windows_version: 9.2.0.0
cee_windows_product_id: '{81F4A925-A885-4F58-8907-641BC7E82B99}'

# The account the EMC CAVA service (Windows service name "EMC Checker
# Server") logs on as. OPTIONAL, and both go together or neither does —
# cee_preflight rejects an account with no password.
#
# This is the vendor's "Complete the CEE installation for Windows Server"
# step (Using the Common Event Enabler on Windows Platforms 9.x rev 24,
# pp11-12): the service should run as a domain account holding the rights
# to set up CAVA and CEPA server accounts, not as LocalSystem. It requires
# a domain-joined host, so it is off by default.
#
# Leaving both unset does NOT reset the service to LocalSystem — it leaves
# whatever account the service already has. A host configured by hand
# cannot be silently reverted by a playbook run that omits these.
#
# cee_configure grants SeServiceLogonRight to the account before setting
# it. That is required: the Services MMC grants it implicitly when an
# operator picks an account there, win_service does not, and without it the
# service accepts the credentials and then fails to start at next boot with
# a logon error rather than a privilege one.
#
# Measured caveat worth knowing before you decide you need this: CEE's
# outbound leg to an HTTP consumer works as LocalSystem on a workgroup
# host — verified continuously against cee-exporter. The guide frames the
# domain account around Microsoft RPC consumers; an HTTP consumer may not
# need it. See docs/cee-9-3-windows-bring-up.md.
#
# ansible-vault this file, or reference a vaulted variable. Gitignored is
# not encrypted.
#
# cee_windows_service_account: DIAB\svc-cepa
# cee_windows_service_password: !vault |
#         $ANSIBLE_VAULT;1.1;AES256
#         ...
